        heading: 'Celie’s letters to God',
        body:
          'The novel opens with a threat rather than a greeting. Celie, a poor Black girl of fourteen in rural Georgia, has been told never to tell anybody but God what is happening to her, so she writes to God instead. She is raped repeatedly by the man she believes to be her father, and the two babies she bears are taken from her before she can learn where they have gone. Her letters record all of this in short, unadorned sentences, as though grief were simply the weather.\n\nHer one comfort is her younger sister Nettie, who is quick and bookish and whom Celie protects at every cost to herself. When a widower the girls know only as Mister comes courting Nettie, Celie is handed over in her place, along with a cow, to keep house for him and raise his unruly children. Nettie escapes Mister’s advances soon afterward and vanishes, and the sisters’ promise to write becomes the thread on which Celie’s hope hangs.\n\nThese opening letters establish the novel’s central deprivation. Celie has no money, no schooling to speak of, no protection, and above all no listener, and her way of surviving is to make herself into wood so the blows land on something that cannot feel. Yet the letters themselves argue against that resignation, since someone is clearly paying close attention, remembering everything, and setting it down.',
