        heading: 'The women who change her',
        body:
          'Change reaches Celie through other women rather than through any man. Sofia, who marries Celie’s stepson Harpo, is broad, strong, and entirely unwilling to be ruled, and when Celie, poisoned by her own submission, advises Harpo to beat his wife, Sofia confronts her directly. All her life she has had to fight, she tells Celie, listing the brothers and uncles and cousins she fought before she ever met a husband. The confession shames Celie into her first honest apology and her first glimpse that obedience is a choice she has been making.\n\nShug Avery arrives next, first as a photograph Celie studies like scripture and then as a sick, sharp-tongued blues singer whom Mister brings home and Celie nurses back to health. Shug teaches her that her body is her own, sings a song from a juke-joint stage and names it after her, and treats Celie’s tenderness as something worth having rather than something to be tolerated. Under Shug’s attention, Celie begins for the first time to want things.\n\nIt is Shug who makes the novel’s great discovery. Suspecting that Mister is hiding something, she finds years of Nettie’s letters locked in his trunk, proof that the sister Celie has mourned is alive and working as a missionary in Africa, helping to raise the very children who were taken away. Reading them, Celie also learns that the man who abused her was her stepfather rather than her father, and her long-frozen grief finally turns into rage.',
