        heading: 'Unflinching subject matter',
        body:
          'Walker refuses euphemism. The first page contains a rape described in a child’s vocabulary, and the novel goes on to include incest, the forced surrender of newborns, routine domestic beating, and the racial violence that falls on Sofia with the full weight of the state behind it. None of it is staged for shock. The plainness is the point, because Celie does not yet possess a language for outrage and can only report what happened.\n\nThe same directness governs the book’s treatment of desire. Shug explains Celie’s own body to her, and their love is written with warmth rather than apology, which is one reason the novel has been challenged in American schools for decades. Walker plainly regards the two kinds of frankness as inseparable: a book that tells the truth about what was done to Celie owes her the truth about what she later enjoys.\n\nWhat saves the novel from despair is the shape of the whole. Walker spends her darkness early and her light late, so the reader who watched Celie hand over her children also gets to watch her hand out wages, sit at her own table, and meet her sister at the gate. The happiness of the ending is not a consolation tacked on; it has been earned page by page.',
