    aboutAuthor:
      'Alice Walker was born in 1944 in rural Georgia, the youngest of eight children in a sharecropping family, and the countryside of *The Color Purple* is essentially the one she grew up in. A childhood accident left her blind in one eye, an injury she has written about as the beginning of a lifelong habit of watching closely and reading everything within reach. She studied at Spelman College and then at Sarah Lawrence, and she spent part of the 1960s working in the civil rights movement in Mississippi.\n\nWalker coined the word "womanist" to describe a feminism grounded in the history and culture of Black women rather than borrowed from anyone else, and that idea supplies the novel’s politics: Celie is not presented as a case study but as a life told from the inside. Walker also devoted years to recovering the reputation of Zora Neale Hurston, whose work had fallen out of print, and she searched out and marked Hurston’s unmarked grave in Florida. That act of literary rescue belongs to the same conviction that drives the novel, namely that Black women’s voices survive only when someone takes the trouble to keep them.\n\n*The Color Purple* appeared in 1982 and won both the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction and the National Book Award, making Walker the first Black woman to receive the fiction Pulitzer. She has gone on to publish novels, essays, and poetry across a long career, while the book itself has led a second life on screen and on stage. It also remains among the most frequently challenged titles in American schools, a distinction that says less about the novel than about what it insists on saying out loud.',
