    aboutAuthor:
      'Colson Whitehead was born in 1969 and grew up in New York City, and he began his career writing for a weekly newspaper before publishing his first novel in the late 1990s. His books are famous for refusing to settle into a single mode: he has written a detective story about elevator inspectors, a zombie novel set in a quarantined Manhattan, a coming-of-age summer in Sag Harbor, and a pair of Harlem crime novels. That restlessness is part of why *The Underground Railroad* does not read like a conventional historical novel.\n\nWhitehead has said that the central idea came to him many years before he wrote the book, and that he waited until he felt equal to it. The starting point was the ordinary childhood assumption that the underground railroad was a real train, and the decision to honor that image rather than correct it gave him a structure capable of carrying far more than one escape. He has also described wanting each state to work like a separate floor of a building, an approach that turned the novel into a survey of American arrangements rather than a single journey.\n\nPublished in 2016, the novel won the National Book Award and then the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction, and Whitehead won a second fiction Pulitzer a few years later for *The Nickel Boys*, a distinction shared by only a handful of American writers. He continues to publish widely and is generally regarded as one of the defining novelists of his era, admired equally for his sentences and for his willingness to keep changing shape.',
