    aboutAuthor:
      'Junot Díaz was born in the Dominican Republic in 1968 and moved to central New Jersey as a young child, joining a father who had emigrated ahead of the family. The immigrant neighborhoods of that state, and the constant traffic back and forth to Santo Domingo, supply the geography of nearly everything he has written. He was a heavy reader of science fiction and comics as a boy, an enthusiasm he has never treated as a phase, and it shows in a novel that discusses Trujillo and Tolkien in the same breath.\n\nHis first book, the story collection *Drown*, appeared in 1996 and made him famous quickly for a voice that mixed Spanish and English without apology and wrote about working-class Dominican-American life with neither sentimentality nor explanation. The novel that followed took him more than ten years, a delay he has spoken about candidly, and it grew out of both his own family history and long research into the Trujillo dictatorship.\n\n*The Brief Wondrous Life of Oscar Wao* was published in 2007 and won the National Book Critics Circle Award and the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction, and Díaz was later awarded a MacArthur Fellowship. He has taught creative writing for many years and has published further fiction featuring Yunior, and his work is credited with widening what an American literary voice is permitted to sound like.',
