        name: 'Kirsten Raymonde',
        role: 'An actress with the Traveling Symphony',
        motivation:
          'Kirsten wants to keep performing, which in her world means walking hundreds of miles a year so that scattered settlements can hear Shakespeare spoken aloud. She is also driven by an archivist’s hunger for the vanished world, hoarding celebrity magazine clippings about a man she barely remembers meeting and asking every stranger what they still have. Underneath both is a fear of being alone, which is why the Symphony matters to her more than any single person in it. She has learned to survive and does not consider that sufficient.',
        arc:
          'She begins as a child in the wings of a theatre on the last ordinary night in history and grows into a hardened traveler who carries knives, has killed when she had to, and has the tattoos to record it. The pursuit by the Prophet strips away the Symphony’s routines and leaves her walking alone through country she does not know, testing whether her devotion to the work can survive without the company that made it possible. Meeting the Prophet, she discovers that the two of them were shaped by the same comic book and the same absent father figure, and that the difference between them is what each chose to do with the story. She ends the novel at a window with a telescope, looking at electric light in the distance, still intending to go on performing.',
