    aboutAuthor:
      'Emily St. John Mandel was born in 1979 and grew up on a small island off the coast of British Columbia, in a household without television and some distance from anything resembling a city. She left home as a teenager to study contemporary dance in Toronto and worked in dance and in office jobs before turning to fiction, and traces of both backgrounds run through her work: an interest in performers and their discipline, and an eye for the strange half-life of corporate rooms.\n\nHer first three novels were literary thrillers with modest readerships, and *Station Eleven*, published in 2014, was the fourth. She has said it grew from thinking about how completely modern life depends on infrastructure most people never see, and that she set out to write as much about the beauty of the contemporary world as about its disappearance, which is why the novel keeps pausing to admire electric light, airplanes, and the ordinary miracle of a lit city at night.\n\nThe book was a finalist for the National Book Award, won the Arthur C. Clarke Award, and became a major bestseller before being adapted into an acclaimed television series, and its readership grew again after 2020, when its subject acquired an immediacy nobody had wanted. Mandel has continued to write novels that share characters and preoccupations with this one, returning to questions of time, memory, art, and the ways separate lives turn out to be connected.',
